WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.579
Haze, smoke, fog, rain, snow, low cloud, glare, and low light reduce visual contrast and depth cues.

00:00:07.579 --> 00:00:16.796
They can affect the operator's view of the aircraft, surrounding airspace, terrain, and obstacles even when the camera feed appears bright.

00:00:16.796 --> 00:00:23.555
Visual-line-of-sight requirements and operational awareness apply to the whole scene, not only an aircraft light.

00:00:23.555 --> 00:00:31.134
If the pilot cannot reliably judge position, orientation, flight path, or hazards, the mission should not continue.

00:00:31.134 --> 00:00:32.978
Sensors have limits.

00:00:32.978 --> 00:00:38.098
Automatic exposure may brighten a scene while increasing noise or blur.

00:00:38.098 --> 00:00:41.171
Precipitation and wind can destabilize footage.

00:00:41.171 --> 00:00:47.111
Haze and smoke can remove fine detail or change apparent color and distance.

00:00:47.111 --> 00:00:51.822
Data quality should be part of go or no-go criteria.

00:00:51.822 --> 00:00:55.714
Define the detail and accuracy needed before flight.

00:00:55.714 --> 00:01:03.292
If conditions cannot produce evidence suitable for the decision, taking the flight adds risk without mission value.

00:01:03.292 --> 00:01:09.232
A visible aircraft light does not prove that the surrounding route is visible.

00:01:09.232 --> 00:01:16.811
The operator must still recognize attitude, trajectory, terrain, obstacles, and nearby airspace well enough for controlled flight.

00:01:16.811 --> 00:01:26.438
Review a sample when safe and watch for lens obstruction, focus loss, vibration, motion blur, compression, low contrast, or inconsistent mapping overlap.

00:01:26.438 --> 00:01:31.149
Avoid interpreting detail that the sensor did not capture reliably.

00:01:31.149 --> 00:01:36.680
Document visibility, weather, lighting, wind, sensor settings, and limitations for later analysis.

00:01:36.680 --> 00:01:43.029
A report should explain whether poor conditions could hide features or create apparent changes.
